":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The answer gets the central distinction right. APs create and redeem creation units directly with the fund in the primary market. Market makers post continuous two-sided quotes and provide secondary-market liquidity. It also correctly says market makers need not have an AP agreement, that many firms do both roles, and that market makers who are also APs use creation and redemption to offset inventory and hedge. There are two gaps. First, it omits lead market makers (LMMs): it never says an LMM is designated for a specific ETF with quoting commitments. Second, it implies only market makers who are APs can manage inventory through creation and redemption. It leaves out that non-AP market makers do this by working through APs. Both are substantive omissions from the key points, but there is no material factual error.\"}","ttft_ms":2725,"type":"result","duration_ms":4932,"uuid":"93975a3c-508d-4e34-b759-a840ea7550f5","ttft_stream_ms":556,"time_to_request_ms":67,"first_content_frame_ms":556,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
